text:
jamie: Heads up — running into a weird one in the Node sampleapp: request-id disappears in child process logs when we fork workers. Repro steps + logs below.

mike: Can you paste the minimal repro and Node version?

jamie: Node 18.16.0. Minimal flow: parent HTTP handler generates reqId, sets process.env.REDWOOD_LOG_LEVEL=debug and then forks worker to handle CPU work. Worker initializes our logging hook and OpenTelemetry at startup, but logs from worker show no reqId and log level is INFO even though REDWOOD_LOG_LEVEL=debug in parent.

jamie: Repro snippet:

```js
// parent.js
const { fork } = require('child_process');
const reqId = 'req-' + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2);
process.env.REDWOOD_LOG_LEVEL = 'debug';
fork('./worker.js'); // no env override
// worker.js
// logging init reads process.env.REDWOOD_LOG_LEVEL and tries to grab context from OpenTelemetry
console.log('worker start - level=' + process.env.REDWOOD_LOG_LEVEL + ' req=' + process.env.REQUEST_ID);
```

jamie: parent log: "parent start - level=debug req=req-abc123"
jamie: worker log: "worker start - level=INFO req=undefined"

sarah: Interesting — child_process.fork will inherit environment, but the logging lib initializes before the child receives any runtime IPC or parent-side context. Also OpenTelemetry context (async hooks) won't carry across a fork. If your SDK expects async context to bootstrap request-id, that will fail in a forked worker.

mike: Do we see this only when parent toggles REDWOOD_LOG_LEVEL dynamically? Or always?

jamie: Always in our tests when worker does heavy initialization. Looks like the logging init does a fallback to 'INFO' if it can't find context/ENV at the exact moment of init (race).

sarah: Workarounds: 1) pass REQUEST_ID in the fork env: fork('./worker.js', { env: { ...process.env, REQUEST_ID: reqId } }) — ensures child has value synchronously. 2) child can read initial value from process.env and use it as root context before OTEL init. 3) parent can spawn worker earlier and send reqId over IPC, but that has a race (child init may run before message arrives).

jamie: I tried child.send({ reqId }) but half the time the worker already logged and init completed. Setting env works but feels heavy (leaks reqId into env for the life of the process). Concerned about passing sensitive IDs via env for long-lived workers.

mike: Agreed. SDK-level mitigation idea: have the log hook check process.env for REDWOOD_REQUEST_ID or a short-lived sentinel (e.g., REDWOOD_INJECT_REQID) and prefer that during init. Also allow a tiny window (e.g., wait up to 50ms for an IPC "init-context" message before finalizing log level/context) — configurable.

ops-bot: ticket created: DE-3784 forked-worker reqid race (link: https://redwood.internal/DE-3784) :white_check_mark:

sarah: Proposed small change to DX SDK (pseudo):

```js
// in logging init
const envReq = process.env.REDWOOD_REQUEST_ID || process.env.REQUEST_ID;
if (envReq) setRootContext({ requestId: envReq });
// fall back to OTEL/async context if present
// optionally: if process.env.REDWOOD_WAIT_FOR_IPC === '1' then await ipcMessage(50ms)
```

sarah: This covers the common fork case without forcing env usage everywhere; env can be set by parent when spawning for single-request workers.

jamie: Sounds good. I'll open a PR with: (a) env-pref fallback, (b) small configurable init-wait for an IPC message, (c) unit test that forks a child and asserts logs contain reqId. I'll avoid defaulting to long env lifetimes.

mike: Please add a small e2e in the sampleapp folder showing both approaches (env and IPC) so solutions are documented for customers. Also add a note in onboarding docs about fork semantics and async context.

jamie: Will do. Thanks all. :rocket:

sarah: If anyone on mobile hits this, short-term fix: set REQUEST_ID in fork env or avoid heavy init in worker before IPC handshake.

mike: Added to roadmap for SDK parity across runtimes (we should make this behavior consistent in Python/Go too).

jamie: Opening PR now, linking DE-3784.
